At launch, the expo-maps announcement described the library as an alpha: “With SDK 53, we are releasing our alpha version of expo-maps.” The SDK 58 reference page shows no status label. Check the[expo-maps reference](https://docs.expo.dev/versions/latest/sdk/maps/) for the current status before you ship. Expo’s[react-native-maps page](https://docs.expo.dev/versions/latest/sdk/map-view/) covers the other option.


## Install expo-maps and Create a Development Build


expo-maps needs two things: the package and a native binary (a development build) that includes it. Install the package with the Expo CLI so the version matches your SDK:


```text
npx   expo   install   expo-maps   expo-location   expo-dev-client
npx   expo   run:ios        # or: npx expo run:android
```


` expo-location` supplies the user’s coordinates later in this guide.` expo-dev-client` turns the binary into a[development build](https://docs.expo.dev/develop/development-builds/introduction/) . The expo-maps docs only describe running it in a development build, so use one. You can also build in the cloud with EAS. For a ready-made project,` npx create-expo-app --example with-maps` starts with expo-maps already installed and configured.


## Add the Android Google Maps API Key


Apple Maps works without a key. Google Maps on Android needs a Google Cloud API key with the Maps SDK for Android enabled, added to app.json, and then a rebuild of the app.


1. Create a Google Cloud project and enable **Maps SDK for Android** .
2. [Create an API key](https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/android-sdk/get-api-key) and restrict it to Android apps using your package name and the SHA-1 fingerprint of your signing certificate. For EAS builds,` eas credentials` shows the fingerprint.
3. Add the key and the location permission plugin to app.json:


```text
{
"  expo  "  : {
"  android  "  : {
"  package  "  :   "  com.example.places  "  ,
"  config  "  : {
"  googleMaps  "  : {   "  apiKey  "  :   "  YOUR_ANDROID_MAPS_KEY  "   }
}
},
"  plugins  "  : [
[
"  expo-maps  "  ,
{
"  requestLocationPermission  "  :   true  ,
"  locationPermission  "  :   "  Allow $(PRODUCT_NAME) to show your position on the map  "
}
]
]
}
}
```


1. Rebuild with` npx expo run:android` . A JavaScript reload does not pick up changes to native config.


In the plugin options,` requestLocationPermission` is a boolean that adds permissions to AndroidManifest.xml and Info.plist, and` locationPermission` sets the iOS-only usage message. app.json can’t read environment variables. To keep the key out of source control, move the same` android.config.googleMaps.apiKey` path into` app.config.js` and read the key from` process.env` there.


## Show a Map on Both Platforms


expo-maps has no cross-platform component, so write a small wrapper that chooses` AppleMaps.View` or` GoogleMaps.View` based on` Platform.OS` and passes both the same` cameraPosition` . A` CameraPosition` is an object with` coordinates` and` zoom` :


```text
import   {   Platform  ,   Text   }   from   '  react-native  '  ;
import   {   AppleMaps  ,   GoogleMaps   }   from   '  expo-maps  '  ;


const   START   =   {   coordinates  :   {   latitude  :   51.5074  ,   longitude  :   -  0.1278   },   zoom  :   12   };


export   function   BasicMap  () {
if (  Platform  .  OS   ===   '  ios  '  )   return   <  AppleMaps.View   style  =  {  {   flex  :   1   }  }   cameraPosition  =  {START} />  ;
if (  Platform  .  OS   ===   '  android  '  )   return   <  GoogleMaps.View   style  =  {  {   flex  :   1   }  }   cameraPosition  =  {START} />  ;
return   <  Text  >Maps are only available on Android and iOS.</  Text  >  ;
}
```


The final branch handles web and any other platform, where neither component exists.


## Add Markers from One Data Array


Both` AppleMaps.View` and` GoogleMaps.View` take a` markers` array, and both marker types accept` id` ,` title` and` coordinates` . That means one mapping function can feed both platforms. Add platform-only fields, such as` tintColor` on Apple or` snippet` on Google, by spreading them onto the shared marker:


```text
type   Place   =   {   id  :   string  ;   title  :   string  ;   latitude  :   number  ;   longitude  :   number   };


export   const   PLACES  :   Place  []   =   [
{   id  :   '  office  '  ,   title  :   '  Office  '  ,   latitude  :   51.5154  ,   longitude  :   -  0.141   },
{   id  :   '  cafe  '  ,   title  :   '  Cafe  '  ,   latitude  :   51.5113  ,   longitude  :   -  0.1198   },
];


export   const   toMarker   =   (  p  :   Place  )   =>   ({
id  :   p  .  id  ,
title  :   p  .  title  ,
coordinates  :   {   latitude  :   p  .  latitude  ,   longitude  :   p  .  longitude   },
});


// iOS:     markers={PLACES.map((p) => ({ ...toMarker(p), tintColor: '#e5484d' }))}
// Android: markers={PLACES.map((p) => ({ ...toMarker(p), snippet: 'Open now' }))}
```


expo-maps can’t render React components as markers. For custom pins, it supports image icons loaded with` useImage` from expo-image.


## How Do You Show and Centre on the User’s Location?


Showing the user’s location in expo-maps takes three steps: request location permission, show the location dot once permission is granted, and move the camera when the coordinates arrive. The permission has to be both declared in the build and requested at runtime before the dot can appear. The` useLocationPermissions` hook from expo-maps returns the current status and a function to request it.


Task` AppleMaps.View`` GoogleMaps.View`


Location dot` properties.isMyLocationEnabled`` properties.isMyLocationEnabled` plus` userLocation`


Follow the user Call` setCameraPosition`` userLocation.followUserLocation`


Animation duration Not supported` duration` in ms


Only enable the dot after permission is granted. A[reported Android issue](https://github.com/expo/expo/issues/49198) showed that turning` isMyLocationEnabled` on without the permission crashed the app on Android, while iOS ignored it. A fix was merged into Expo’s main branch on 4 November 2026, but older expo-maps releases don’t have it, and gating the dot on permission works on every version. The issue also points out that the config plugin’s` requestLocationPermission` option only declares the permissions at build time and never requests anything at runtime.


In expo-maps, the` cameraPosition` prop is documented as the initial camera position of the map, so changing it in state isn’t the documented way to move the camera. Call` setCameraPosition` on a ref instead. On iOS, animation duration is not supported, and the returned promise resolves after the camera update is scheduled, not when its animation finishes.


```text
import   {   useEffect  ,   useRef  ,   useState  ,   type   ComponentRef   }   from   '  react  '  ;
import   {   Platform  ,   Text  ,   View   }   from   '  react-native  '  ;
import   {   AppleMaps  ,   GoogleMaps  ,   useLocationPermissions   }   from   '  expo-maps  '  ;
import *   as   Location   from   '  expo-location  '  ;
import   {   PLACES  ,   toMarker   }   from   '  ./places  '  ;


type   Coords   =   {   latitude  :   number  ;   longitude  :   number   };


export   function   PlacesMap  ({   onSelect   }  :   {   onSelect  :   (  id  :   string  )   =>   void   }) {
const   appleRef   =   useRef  <  ComponentRef  <  typeof   AppleMaps  .  View  >>(  null  );
const   [  permission  ,   requestPermission  ]   =   useLocationPermissions  ();
const   [  me  ,   setMe  ]   =   useState  <  Coords   |   null  >(  null  );
const   granted   =   !!  permission  ?.  granted  ;


useEffect  (()   =>   {
if (  permission   &&   !  permission  .  granted   &&   permission  .  canAskAgain  )   requestPermission  ();
}, [  permission  ]);


useEffect  (()   =>   {
if (  !  granted  )   return  ;
Location  .  getCurrentPositionAsync  ().  then  (({   coords   })   =>   {
const   here   =   {   latitude  :   coords  .  latitude  ,   longitude  :   coords  .  longitude   };
setMe  (  here  );
appleRef  .  current  ?.  setCameraPosition  ({   coordinates  :   here  ,   zoom  :   14   });   // iOS only; no duration
});
}, [  granted  ]);


const   handleMarker   =   (  m  :   { id  ?:   string   })   =>   m  .  id   &&   onSelect  (  m  .  id  );
const   denied   =   permission   &&   !  permission  .  granted   &&   !  permission  .  canAskAgain  ;


const   map   =
Platform  .  OS   ===   '  ios  '   ?   (
<  AppleMaps.View
ref  =  {appleRef}
style  =  {  {   flex  :   1   }  }
cameraPosition  =  {START}
markers  =  {  PLACES  .  map  ((  p  )   =>   ({   ...  toMarker  (  p  ),   tintColor  :   '  #e5484d  '   }))  }
properties  =  {  {   isMyLocationEnabled  :   granted   }  }
onMarkerClick  =  {handleMarker}   // iOS 18+
/>
)   :   Platform  .  OS   ===   '  android  '   ?   (
<  GoogleMaps.View
style  =  {  {   flex  :   1   }  }
cameraPosition  =  {START}
markers  =  {  PLACES  .  map  (  toMarker  )  }
properties  =  {  {   isMyLocationEnabled  :   granted   }  }
userLocation  =  {me   ?   {   coordinates  :   me  ,   followUserLocation  :   true   }   :   undefined  }
onMarkerClick  =  {handleMarker}
/>
)   :   (
<  Text  >Maps are only available on Android and iOS.</  Text  >
);


return   (
<  View   style  =  {  {   flex  :   1   }  }>
{map}
{denied   &&   <  Text  >Location is off. Enable it in Settings to see where you are.</  Text  >}
</  View  >
);
}
```


[Location.getCurrentPositionAsync](https://docs.expo.dev/versions/latest/sdk/location/) supplies the coordinates. On Android,` followUserLocation` keeps the camera on the user, so the code only calls` setCameraPosition` on iOS. If permission is denied for good, the map stays at` START` and a non-blocking message appears below it.


## Respond to Marker Taps with onMarkerClick


To handle marker taps in expo-maps, pass` onMarkerClick` to` AppleMaps.View` or` GoogleMaps.View` . The callback receives the tapped marker, so you can read its` id` and find the matching record in your data. On Android it works on every supported version. On iOS, Expo’s docs mark it **iOS 18.0+** , and the same limit applies to the other tap callbacks and to programmatic selection with` selectMarker` and` selectAnnotation` .


On iOS 17 devices the map still renders, but tapping a marker does nothing in JavaScript. If tap-driven UI is required, set` ios.deploymentTarget` to 18.0 via` expo-build-properties` . Otherwise, add a list below the map that drives the same selection state, so iOS 17 users can still reach each place.


## Conclusion


expo-maps covers the common map screen with native components: Apple Maps on iOS, Google Maps on Android, one shared data array and a short` Platform.OS` branch. Next, rebuild with your Android key and test on a real iOS 17 device and an iOS 18 device. That is where the tap and permission differences show up. After release,[session replay for React Native](https://blog.openreplay.com/session-replay-for-react-native/) shows the failures you can’t see in logs, such as users panning around looking for a pin or dismissing the location prompt. Watching those sessions tells you whether the map flow works for real users.


## FAQs


Why does the Google map work in development but stay blank in the Play Store build?


The API key is usually restricted to the wrong SHA-1 fingerprint. With Play App Signing, Google re-signs the release, so the installed app carries Google's app signing certificate instead of your upload or debug key. Copy the app signing SHA-1 from the App integrity page in Play Console and add it to the key's Android app restrictions. Keep your development fingerprint on the key as well.


Can expo-maps draw routes, areas or radius circles?


Yes. Both AppleMaps.View and GoogleMaps.View accept polylines, polygons and circles props. Each prop takes an array of shapes built from coordinates, and a circle takes a center and a radius in metres. expo-maps draws shapes but does not calculate routes. For a driving or walking path, get the route geometry from a directions service and pass the decoded coordinates as a polyline.


How do I switch expo-maps to a satellite view?


Set mapType inside the properties prop. On AppleMaps.View, use the AppleMapsMapType enum: STANDARD, IMAGERY (satellite only) or HYBRID (satellite with roads and labels). On GoogleMaps.View, use GoogleMapsMapType: NORMAL, SATELLITE, HYBRID or TERRAIN. The value names differ by platform, so a shared wrapper should map one setting, such as 'satellite', to IMAGERY on iOS and SATELLITE on Android.


Does location permission granted through expo-maps also cover expo-location?


Yes. Android and iOS grant location access to the app as a whole, not to a single library. Once the user accepts the foreground prompt from the useLocationPermissions hook in expo-maps, expo-location sees the same permission, so getCurrentPositionAsync runs without a second prompt. Background location is a separate grant on both platforms and needs its own request through expo-location.


